{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI in Education","title":"Responsible Prompting With Students","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting responsible prompting with students to TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"05","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/03/04"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Responsible Prompting With Students\n\n![Responsible Prompting With Students textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-05-responsible-prompting-with-students.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen students use AI in your classroom, the prompt is the lesson. How a student frames a request to a model reveals and shapes their thinking: a vague prompt outsources the whole task, while a precise prompt requires the student to already understand the problem well enough to specify it. This means prompting is not a side skill to be taught after the content; for many tasks it is the cognitive work itself, and teaching it well is teaching the subject.\n\nThis section matters because the two biggest risks of student AI use both live in the prompt. The first is privacy: students will type anything into a chatbot, including classmates' names, their own sensitive situations, and protected information, with no sense that the text leaves the room and may train a commercial system. The second is intellectual: a student who pastes \"do my essay\" learns nothing, while a student who learns to specify, critique, and verify learns more than the assignment alone would teach. A teacher who can coach responsible prompting turns a tool that could replace thinking into one that scaffolds it, and protects students from a privacy mistake they do not yet know how to avoid.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher lets her fifth graders use a class chatbot to get help with a research project. She circles the room and reads over shoulders. One student has typed: \"My friend Destiny is being mean to me and her dad just got arrested, what should I do.\" Another typed: \"Write my whole report on volcanoes.\" A third typed nothing useful for ten minutes, then \"tell me about volcanoes,\" and is now copying a paragraph word for word.\n\nThree students, three different failures, all in the prompt. The first poured another child's family information and a personal conflict into a commercial system that retains text and is not a counselor. The second outsourced the entire task and will learn nothing. The third does not yet know how to ask a question that produces useful, checkable help. None of these is solved by banning the tool or by a one-time \"be safe online\" reminder. Each needs the teacher to coach the prompt itself: strip the private information, narrow the vague request, and add a verification step. The prompt is where responsibility is taught or lost.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nResponsible prompting with students has three components, and they are best taught as a routine students internalize, not as rules posted on a wall.\n\n- **Protect the data (input safety).** Anything typed into a public AI tool can leave the room, be stored, and be used to train a commercial model. The non-negotiable rule is that students never enter names of other people, their own identifying or sensitive personal information, or protected data. This is not a vague caution; it is a specific input filter students apply before they hit enter. A request about a classmate's behavior is handled with a human, never a chatbot. Privacy basis: FERPA (20 U.S.C. 1232g; 34 CFR Part 99) governs disclosure of education records, and its 34 CFR 99.31(a)(1) \"school official\" exception defines when a vendor or AI tool may receive them, while COPPA (15 U.S.C. 6501-6506; 16 CFR Part 312, amended 2025) governs data from children under 13; the 2025 COPPA amendments (effective June 23, 2025, with full compliance by April 22, 2026) tighten how operators may retain and monetize that data, which is exactly why a public tool may train on what a student types.\n- **Specify the request.** The difference between \"write the water cycle\" and \"explain the water cycle for sixth graders in five sentences with one everyday example\" is the difference between outsourcing thinking and directing it. Specifying an audience, a length, a purpose, and a constraint forces the student to understand the task first, and it makes the output something they can evaluate rather than just accept. Specification is where the learning hides.\n- **Verify the output (do not copy).** The model can be confidently wrong, so the routine ends with the student checking the key claims against a real source and then writing the result in their own words. Copying the first response is both an integrity failure and a learning failure; the verify step is what keeps the tool a scaffold rather than a substitute.\n\nA simple way to hold the routine: **strip, specify, verify.** Strip private information out, specify the request precisely, verify the output before using it. The teacher's job is to make this sequence automatic, the way \"look both ways\" is automatic, so that it operates even when no one is watching the screen.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student types: Tell me about Maria Lopez in my class who is failing, and write the water cycle for me. Which strip-specify-verify move is the most urgent first action?\" explanation=\"Strip comes first and is non-negotiable: a named classmate plus a sensitive judgment about her must never enter a public tool, because the text can be stored and used to train a commercial model. The behavior question goes to a human; specification and verification address only the legitimate academic part afterward.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Specify the water-cycle request first to improve output quality\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Strip the named classmate out and route the behavior question to a human\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Verify the output, since that is where learning happens\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing; the prompt is fine as written\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nUnderneath the routine is a pedagogical point: a well-specified prompt is evidence of understanding. When you coach a student from a vague prompt to a precise one, you are not teaching tool use; you are walking them through the analysis the assignment was supposed to provoke. The prompt becomes a thinking instrument.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA seventh grader working on a history assignment types: \"tell me about World War 2.\" The output is a generic, sprawling summary he starts to copy. The teacher coaches the prompt instead of confiscating the laptop.\n\nFirst, strip: there is no private data here, so that filter passes. Next, specify: she asks him what his actual assignment question is. It is \"why did the US enter the war.\" Together they rewrite the prompt to \"Explain the main reasons the United States entered World War 2 in 1941, for a seventh-grade reader, in three short paragraphs, and list the two most important causes.\" Notice that to write this prompt he had to recall his own assignment, identify his audience, and commit to a structure; the act of specifying did real cognitive work the vague prompt skipped entirely.\n\nFinally, verify: the improved output names Pearl Harbor and Lend-Lease. The teacher has him check Lend-Lease against the textbook, where he discovers the AI slightly overstated a date. He corrects it and writes his paragraph in his own words, citing the textbook for the date rather than the chatbot. The finished work is his, the tool accelerated the framing rather than replacing the thinking, and he just practiced the exact habit, strip-specify-verify, that will keep his AI use responsible when the teacher is not standing there. The coaching took four minutes and taught both the content and the method.\n\n## Worked Decision: Coaching a Student Prompt\n\nFollow a teacher's annotated before/after judgment on a sixth-grader's research prompt. The transcript is real, and the teacher edits it with the student.\n\n1. **Before (vague, unsafe prompt):** Student types: \"Tell me about Maria Lopez in my class who is failing, and write the water cycle for me.\" The prompt names a classmate and outsources the whole task.\n2. **Privacy edit:** Annotate the identifier. The teacher removes the named classmate entirely, because student names must never go into a public tool. The behavior question is handled offline, not in the chatbot.\n3. **Specification edit:** Annotate the vagueness. Together they rewrite the task prompt to \"Explain the water cycle for sixth graders in five sentences with one everyday example,\" naming audience, purpose, and constraint.\n4. **After (verify routine):** The student reads the output, checks the evaporation claim against the science textbook, finds one oversimplified sentence, and refines the prompt. The teacher records: \"Accepted the structure, rejected sentence three, verified against the text.\" The student wrote the final summary in her own words.\n5. **Evidence trail:** The revision is justified by research on AI and education datafication (Perrotta & Selwyn, 2019) and current K-12 guidance on generative-AI data retention (Future of Privacy Forum, 2024) for input privacy, and by **prompting_practice** (specify and verify). The rejected first output and the removed identifier are recorded with reasons.\n\nStep 1 to step 4 captures the section's lesson: responsible prompting protects student data, specifies the request, and verifies the output rather than copying the first response.\n\nNow coach the prompt yourself. In the branching scenario below, a sixth grader types an unsafe, vague prompt; your job is to apply strip, specify, and verify in the right order rather than confiscate the laptop.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Coaching a student prompt in real time\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"A student types: Tell me about Maria Lopez who is failing, and write the water cycle for me. What is your first coaching move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Specify the water-cycle request to get better output\",\"next\":\"premature\",\"feedback\":\"Quality matters, but a named classmate is in the prompt; safety comes first.\"},{\"label\":\"Strip the classmate name and route the behavior question to a human\",\"next\":\"stripped\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The most important prompting decision here is what not to type.\"},{\"label\":\"Confiscate the laptop and ban the tool for the day\",\"next\":\"ban\",\"feedback\":\"Banning skips the coaching; the student learns nothing transferable.\"}]},\"premature\":{\"prompt\":\"Before sending, you notice the classmate name is still in the prompt. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Remove the name and handle the conflict offline first\",\"next\":\"stripped\",\"feedback\":\"You applied the strip filter before anything was sent.\"}]},\"ban\":{\"prompt\":\"The student still has a legitimate water-cycle task. How do you re-engage?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Coach the prompt: strip the name, then specify the request\",\"next\":\"stripped\",\"feedback\":\"Coaching the prompt teaches the habit the ban would have skipped.\"}]},\"stripped\":{\"prompt\":\"The name is gone. The remaining request is still vague: write the water cycle for me. What do you do next?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Specify: audience, length, purpose, and one constraint\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Specifying is where the student does the assignment thinking.\"},{\"label\":\"Let the student copy whatever the tool returns\",\"next\":\"copy\",\"feedback\":\"Copying the first response is both an integrity and a learning failure.\"}]},\"verify\":{\"prompt\":\"The improved output explains the water cycle in five sentences. What is the final step before the student uses it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Have the student check a key claim against the textbook and rewrite it in their own words\",\"next\":\"responsible\",\"feedback\":\"The verify step keeps the tool a scaffold, not a substitute.\"},{\"label\":\"Submit the output as-is; it reads well\",\"next\":\"copy\",\"feedback\":\"Confident output still needs checking; reading well is not being correct.\"}]},\"copy\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: the student copied unverified output, so the learning and the integrity both lapsed.\",\"choices\":[]},\"responsible\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible outcome: no student data leaked, the request was specified so the student did the thinking, and the work ended checked and student-authored.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen students prompt an AI tool, coach the prompt through the strip-specify-verify routine:\n\n1. **Strip private data first.** Remove names of others, the student's own sensitive information, and any protected data before the prompt is sent. Conflicts and personal crises go to a human, never a chatbot.\n2. **Specify the request.** Push the student to name audience, length, purpose, and constraint, because specifying is where they do the thinking the assignment intends.\n3. **Verify before using.** Have the student check key claims against a real source and write the result in their own words rather than copying.\n4. **Make it a habit, not a poster.** Coach the routine repeatedly until students apply it unprompted, because the goal is responsible use when you are not watching.\n\nA prompt is responsible when it leaks no one's data, directs the tool precisely, and ends in checked, student-authored work.\n\nMatch each routine move to the specific job it does. This keeps strip, specify, and verify from collapsing into a single vague \"be careful.\"\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Strip\",\"definition\":\"Remove names of others and sensitive personal data before sending, and route conflicts to a human\"},{\"term\":\"Specify\",\"definition\":\"Name the audience, length, purpose, and constraint so the student does the assignment thinking\"},{\"term\":\"Verify\",\"definition\":\"Check key claims against a real source and rewrite the result in the student own words\"},{\"term\":\"Make it a habit\",\"definition\":\"Coach the routine repeatedly so students apply it when no one is watching the screen\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent misreading is treating privacy as a one-time announcement rather than a per-prompt input filter; students forget a verbal reminder the moment a real question arises. Another is assuming that detailed prompting only matters for output quality, missing that specification is itself the cognitive work. A third is conflating verification with trust in the tool's confidence; a confident answer still needs checking. Some teachers also treat \"responsible prompting\" as a topic to lecture once, when it is a routine that only sticks through repeated coaching. The deepest misreading is seeing the prompt as a technical detail rather than as the place where both safety and learning are decided.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the routine through a realistic classroom moment. A ninth grader in a health class is using an AI tool for a project and types: \"My cousin has an eating disorder and I want to help, what do I do, also write my paragraph about nutrition.\"\n\nStrip first, and this case is urgent. The student has disclosed sensitive third-party health information and a real personal concern to a commercial chatbot. The teacher's first move is to stop the prompt, explain why that information must not go into the tool, and route the personal question to a counselor, a human who can actually help. This is the privacy filter working in a high-stakes form: the most important prompting decision here is what not to type.\n\nThen, on the legitimate part of the task, specify and verify. They rewrite the academic request to \"Summarize the three macronutrients and one function of each for a ninth-grade health class, in a short paragraph.\" The student reads the output, checks one claim against the class text, finds it accurate, and rewrites the paragraph in his own voice. Same tool, same student, but the routine separated a privacy emergency from a learning task and handled each correctly. The teacher then debriefs the strip step with the whole class, without naming the student, so the lesson generalizes.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic sees the prompt as the unit of both safety and learning, and can diagnose a student's prompt the way a writing teacher diagnoses a thesis. As you read, keep a two-column note with the **prompt as written** on the left and the **strip, specify, or verify move it needs** on the right. A prompt with nothing in the right column is rare; most need at least one.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any student AI interaction:\n\n1. Does the prompt contain data that should never enter a public tool, and what is the human alternative?\n2. Is the request specified enough to require the student's own understanding?\n3. What verification step turns the output into checked, student-authored work?\n4. How will I coach this so the student does it without me next time?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour prompt transcript is strong when it shows the coaching, not just a clean final prompt. Record the original prompt, the strip-specify-verify edits you and the student made, and what was rejected (the named classmate removed, the first copied output discarded) with the reason. The weakest transcripts show a polished prompt appearing from nowhere; the strongest show the messy first attempt and the visible reasoning that made it safe and educational.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"I removed the classmate's name before anything was sent because student data must not enter a public tool, I helped specify the request so the student had to identify her own audience and purpose, and I required her to verify one claim and rewrite the result, so the work and the learning stayed hers.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the routine to a tool that is not a chatbot: an AI image generator a student wants to use for a project. The verify step transforms, because the risk is no longer a false fact but a fabricated or biased depiction, and \"checking against a source\" becomes \"checking whether this image misrepresents real people or events.\" The strip step stays critical (no real classmates' likenesses or names in prompts), and specify still drives quality. Carrying strip-specify-verify into a generative-image context, and correctly re-interpreting what \"verify\" means there, is the test that you hold the principle rather than a single chatbot script.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why a well-specified prompt is itself evidence of student understanding, not just a way to get better output. Use the World War 2 example or one of your own.\" answer=\"To write a specific prompt the student must already grasp the task: in the World War 2 case the student had to recall his actual assignment question (why the US entered the war), identify his audience (a seventh-grade reader), and commit to a structure (three paragraphs, two main causes). Each of those decisions is the analysis the assignment was meant to provoke. A vague prompt skips that thinking and outsources it to the model, so the act of specifying is where the learning happens, which is why coaching specification is teaching the subject, not just tool use.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"prompting_practice\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-05-responsible-prompting-with-students-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**, and to AI-TPACK as the AI-relevant version of the framework for generative tools (Ning et al., 2024).\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 03.05. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_responsible_prompting_with_students\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record, 108(6), 1017-1054. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Al-Abdullatif, A. M. (2024). Modeling teachers' acceptance of generative artificial intelligence use in higher education: The role of AI literacy, intelligent TPACK, and perceived trust. Education Sciences, 14(11), 1209. https://doi.org/10.3390/educsci14111209\n- Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), 20 U.S.C. 1232g; 34 CFR Part 99. U.S. Department of Education. https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/faq/what-ferpa\n- Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule (COPPA), 16 CFR Part 312, final rule amendments. Federal Trade Commission (2025). https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n- Future of Privacy Forum. (2024). Vetting generative AI tools for use in schools. https://fpf.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Ed_AI_legal_compliance.pdf_FInal_OCT24.pdf\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology, 45(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"prompting_practice","stem":"A teacher wants AI help writing a behavior plan and types into a public chatbot: \"Suggest interventions for Jayden Carter, grade 4, diagnosed with ADHD, who hit a peer Tuesday.\" What is the responsible-prompting problem?","options":["The prompt is too short to work.","It pastes identifiable student data (name, grade, diagnosis, incident) into a public tool that may store and train on it.","ADHD interventions cannot be requested from AI.","There is no problem; the detail makes the answer better."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Responsible prompting strips personally identifiable information first. Describe the situation generically rather than entering a named student's record into a public tool.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_pasting_student_pii","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"prompting_practice","stem":"A student types \"write about the water cycle\" and accepts the generic paragraph that returns. Which teacher move best builds responsible prompting?","options":["Tell the student the prompt was fine since the model figured out the topic.","Model adding audience, purpose, and constraints: \"Explain the water cycle for fourth graders in 4 sentences using one everyday example,\" then compare outputs.","Tell the student never to use AI for science.","Have the student lengthen the prompt with random extra words."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Responsible prompting specifies audience, purpose, and constraints. Modeling that specification is the teachable skill, not hoping the model guesses intent.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_vague_prompt_good_enough","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"prompting_practice","stem":"Students use a chatbot to research a local historical figure and paste the first response into their reports. Two reports contain the same invented birthdate. What routine should the teacher have taught?","options":["Always trust the first AI output to save time.","An iterate-and-verify routine: read the output critically, confirm facts against a reliable source, then refine the prompt or correct the claim.","Use a longer prompt so the first answer is always right.","Forbid research entirely."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The first output is a draft. Responsible prompting includes checking claims against sources and iterating, which would have caught the fabricated birthdate.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_first_output_is_answer","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_responsible_prompting_with_students_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"prompting_practice","stem":"A second-grade teacher plans to let seven-year-olds chat freely and unsupervised with a general-purpose AI assistant whose terms of service set a minimum age of 13. What is the most defensible decision?","options":["Proceed, because young students learn fastest with full freedom.","Do not use that tool as designed; the activity violates the age terms and lacks supervision, so use a vetted, age-appropriate tool with teacher-mediated prompts.","Proceed but tell students to lie about their age.","Proceed, because the tool is popular with adults."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Responsible prompting accounts for age, consent, and supervision. A tool whose terms exclude the students' age and that offers no safeguards is not defensible for unsupervised use.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_5_age_appropriateness_ignored","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"27c0d38608abbe1f24909cc7a58e76ae23852f078774e35fefd12c0ee5eb0f73","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}